  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Monday)\nIssued at 158 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe story of the long-term forecast is one of a long-awaited pattern \nchange. Cooler temperatures are expected. Much drier weather will \noccur in northern/western Oklahoma, while rain chances will linger \nin southeast Oklahoma and parts of western north Texas.\n\nAs the trough responsible for this week's system approaches the \npolar jet, it will degenerate into an open wave. However, the \nremnant weakness in the subtropical ridge (still kicking to our west \nand east) will allow our weather to behave more like early October \nshould. The robust cold front on Thursday will keep sweeping through \non Friday, and a reinforcing front is expected early next week as a \n1026 mb high dominates the central US. This will keep temperatures \nat or below seasonal normals as late as next Monday before lee \ntroughing potentially returns.\n\nShowers do linger in the forecast, particularly Friday and Saturday \nin southeast/central Oklahoma where remnant moisture may be \nstronger. The reinforcing front will do the job to dry us all the \nway out: our forecast calls for no mentionable rain chances next \nMonday.\n\nMeister",
